# Spec-Driven Development

## Required gate

- New or changed product behavior requires a related specification in
  `tasks/specs/` with `status: approved`.
- Do not implement a specification with `draft`, `in_review`, or `superseded`
  status.
- Read the installed
  [`spec-driven-development`](../../.agents/skills/profiles/architecture/spec-driven-development/SKILL.md)
  skill before working on a feature specification.

## Responsibilities

- Keep one primary skill profile per deliverable.
- The human is the only approver of a specification.
- Ask the human about missing business and security decisions. Leave those
  items `TBD`.
- Decide missing design and technical details as a senior engineer, record the
  choice in the specification, and promote recurring visual decisions into
  `docs/design.md`.

## Contract hierarchy

- An approved feature specification is the source of truth for that feature.
- `docs/product.md`, `docs/design.md`, `docs/api.md`, and
  `docs/database.md` are durable global contracts, not replacements for a
  feature specification.
- Add a delta to the feature specification first, then update an affected
  global contract as part of the delivery.
- If business scope or security policy is ambiguous, incomplete, or
  conflicts with a global contract, return it to review. Do not infer
  missing business or security decisions.
- If only design or technical how is missing, decide it, record it, and
  continue. Do not ask the human for visual details that follow the
  consolidating design system.

## Intake

- The only request source is a human prompt in an agent conversation.
- Record `source_kind: human_prompt` and
  `source_reference: chat-YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>`.
- Do not create specifications from GitHub Issues, Kanban cards, webhooks,
  or other systems.
